      <span class="section-num">01 —</span>
      <h2>What is Malware Analysis?</h2>
      <p>Malware analysis is the discipline of dissecting malicious software — viruses, trojans, ransomware, spyware, worms, rootkits, and more — to understand <strong>what it does, how it works, who wrote it, and what it wants</strong>. Think of it as a digital autopsy: the patient (your suspect file) is examined methodically until every secret it hides is laid bare.</p>
      <p>Investigators who perform malware analysis are often called <em>malware analysts</em>, <em>reverse engineers</em>, or <em>threat intelligence researchers</em>. Their findings protect millions of users, drive antivirus signature updates, inform incident response, and occasionally attribute attacks to nation-state actors.</p>
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-label">🔍 Analogy</div>
        Imagine finding a mysterious locked box left at your doorstep. Static analysis is shaking it, weighing it, and X-raying it without opening it. Dynamic analysis is opening it in a blast-proof room and watching what flies out.
      </div>
      <p>Malware analysis sits at the intersection of computer science, criminal investigation, and intelligence work. The skill set spans reading assembly language, understanding operating system internals, network protocol analysis, and creative problem-solving when malware actively tries to hide from you.</p>
      <h3>Why Does It Matter?</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Incident Response:</strong> After a breach, analysts determine exactly what the malware stole, changed, or destroyed.</li>
        <li><strong>Threat Intelligence:</strong> Understanding attacker TTPs (Tactics, Techniques, Procedures) helps predict future attacks.</li>
        <li><strong>Detection Rule Creation:</strong> YARA rules, Snort/Suricata signatures, and EDR detections all flow from malware analysis.</li>
        <li><strong>Attribution:</strong> Code style, infrastructure, and language artifacts can link samples to known threat groups.</li>
        <li><strong>Vulnerability Research:</strong> Exploits embedded in malware reveal unpatched flaws needing immediate attention.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 2 -->
    <section id="s2">
      <span class="section-num">02 —</span>
      <h2>A Brief History</h2>
      <p>The story of malware analysis is inseparable from the story of malware itself. It began almost as soon as computers became interconnected.</p>
      <h3>The Early Days — 1980s</h3>
      <p>The <strong>Brain virus</strong> (1986), considered the first PC virus, spread via floppy disk. Analysts at the time simply read the raw bytes on infected disks using hex editors — the first primitive form of static analysis. There were no automated tools, no sandboxes, and no community knowledge base. Everything was manual, slow, and lonely.</p>
      <h3>The Antivirus Era — 1990s</h3>
      <p>As the internet grew, so did malware diversity. Antivirus companies like Symantec, McAfee, and Kaspersky formalized the role of the malware analyst. Analysts reverse-engineered malware in <strong>disassemblers</strong> like SoftICE and IDA Pro (first released in 1991), extracting byte signatures for detection.</p>
      <h3>The Professional Era — 2000s</h3>
      <p>Malware became professional and financially motivated. <strong>Banking trojans</strong> (ZeuS, SpyEye), <strong>botnets</strong> (Conficker, Storm), and <strong>rootkits</strong> (Sony BMG) demanded deeper analysis. Sandboxes like Norman Sandbox and later Cuckoo Sandbox (open-sourced 2011) automated dynamic analysis at scale. Tools like OllyDbg democratised debugging for analysts.</p>
      <h3>The Nation-State Era — 2010s–Present</h3>
      <p><strong>Stuxnet</strong> (discovered 2010) changed everything. It was the first publicly known malware weapon designed to physically destroy industrial equipment. Analysts spent months dissecting its four zero-day exploits, stolen digital certificates, and targeted payload. Nation-state malware like <em>Flame</em>, <em>Duqu</em>, and <em>WannaCry</em> pushed the field to new heights of sophistication.</p>
      <p>Today, <strong>ransomware-as-a-service</strong> cartels, <strong>supply chain attacks</strong> (SolarWinds), and AI-assisted malware generation define the threat landscape. Analysts use an ever-growing toolkit: static disassemblers, emulators, cloud sandboxes, machine learning classifiers, and collaborative threat intelligence platforms.</p>
    </section>

