      <div class="compare-grid">
        <div class="compare-card static">
          <h4>⚗️ Static Analysis</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Examine the file <strong>without running it</strong></li>
            <li>Read strings, imports, exports, headers</li>
            <li>Disassemble machine code to understand logic</li>
            <li>Works even if malware is broken/incomplete</li>
            <li>Reveals code paths that may never execute</li>
            <li>Safe — malware cannot harm your machine</li>
            <li><em>Limitation:</em> packed/encrypted malware resists static analysis</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="compare-card dynamic">
          <h4>🔥 Dynamic Analysis</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Run the malware in a <strong>controlled environment</strong></li>
            <li>Observe real-time API calls, network traffic, file changes</li>
            <li>Defeats packing and obfuscation automatically</li>
            <li>Reveals C2 servers, dropped payloads, persistence</li>
            <li>Shows what actually happens vs what code says</li>
            <li>Risk: malware may detect the sandbox and hide</li>
            <li><em>Limitation:</em> only sees executed code paths</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="callout warn">
        <div class="callout-label">⚠️ Key Insight</div>
        Neither approach is complete alone. Expert analysts combine both: static analysis first to understand structure and identify suspicious features, then dynamic analysis to confirm runtime behaviour. This is called <strong>hybrid analysis</strong>.
      </div>
