    <section id="s4">
      <span class="section-num">04 —</span>
      <h2>Static Analysis — Deep Dive</h2>
      <p>Static analysis is the art of extracting maximum intelligence from a file without ever pressing "Run." It is the safest and often the first step in any analysis workflow.</p>
      <h3>The Levels of Static Analysis</h3>
      <p>Static analysis ranges from quick triage (a few minutes) to deep reverse engineering (days or weeks). Most investigations start shallow and go deeper only as warranted.</p>

      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">1</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h5>File Identification &amp; Hashing</h5>
            <p>Compute MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256 hashes. Identify file type (not just extension). Submit hash to VirusTotal for instant verdict. This alone takes 30 seconds and may answer 70% of cases.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">2</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h5>String Extraction</h5>
            <p>Dump printable ASCII and Unicode strings from the binary. Look for URLs, IP addresses, registry keys, file paths, error messages, and command-line arguments that reveal intent.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">3</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h5>PE Header Analysis</h5>
            <p>For Windows executables, parse the Portable Executable (PE) structure. Examine the import table, export table, section names, timestamps, and resources. This is the malware's "skeleton."</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">4</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h5>Entropy Analysis</h5>
            <p>Calculate per-section entropy (randomness). High entropy (above 7.0) usually means encryption or compression — a strong indicator of packing. Legitimate code sections typically score 5.0–6.5.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">5</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h5>Disassembly &amp; Decompilation</h5>
            <p>Load in IDA Pro, Ghidra, or Binary Ninja. Read the assembly or pseudo-C output. Identify cryptographic routines, anti-analysis tricks, and the main payload logic.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>The PE File Format — The Skeleton of Windows Malware</h3>
      <p>The <strong>Portable Executable (PE)</strong> format is the standard container for Windows executables (.exe, .dll, .sys). Almost all Windows malware is a PE file. Understanding its structure is fundamental to static analysis.</p>

