    <!-- Section 5 -->
    <section id="s5">
      <span class="section-num">05 —</span>
      <h2>Using strings, file, and PE-bear</h2>
      <p>These three tools form the quick-triage trinity of static analysis. Every analyst reaches for them first. They are fast, safe, and surprisingly revealing.</p>

      <h3>The <code>strings</code> Command</h3>
      <p><strong>strings</strong> is a command-line utility (available on Linux, macOS, and Windows via Sysinternals) that scans a binary file and prints every sequence of 4 or more printable characters. It is often the very first command an analyst runs.</p>
      <pre><code># Basic usage on Linux/macOS
strings suspicious.exe

# Show only strings longer than 8 characters
strings -n 8 suspicious.exe

# Include Unicode (UTF-16) strings — critical for modern Windows malware
strings -el suspicious.exe

# Write output to file for review
strings -a suspicious.exe &gt; strings_output.txt</code></pre>
      <h4>What to Look For in String Output</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>URLs and IP addresses:</strong> C2 (Command &amp; Control) server addresses. Example: <code>http://185.220.101.45/update.php</code></li>
        <li><strong>Registry paths:</strong> Persistence mechanisms. Example: <code>SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run</code></li>
        <li><strong>File system paths:</strong> Where malware drops files. Example: <code>C:\Users\%USERNAME%\AppData\Roaming\svchost32.exe</code></li>
        <li><strong>Windows API names:</strong> Hints at capabilities. <code>CreateRemoteThread</code> → process injection; <code>CryptEncrypt</code> → encryption; <code>WSAConnect</code> → networking</li>
        <li><strong>Mutex names:</strong> Used to prevent re-infection, often unique identifiers for the malware family</li>
        <li><strong>Error messages or debug strings:</strong> Sometimes developers forget to strip these, revealing internal variable names and logic</li>
        <li><strong>Base64 blobs:</strong> Encoded configuration or embedded payloads</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="callout warn">
        <div class="callout-label">⚠️ Limitation</div>
        Packed or encrypted malware may show almost no useful strings — just garbage bytes from the compressed payload. But even that tells you something: this file is obfuscated, which warrants deeper investigation.
      </div>

      <h3>The <code>file</code> Command</h3>
      <p>The <code>file</code> command identifies a file's true type by examining its <strong>magic bytes</strong> — the first few bytes of a file that act as a fingerprint — rather than trusting the file extension. Attackers often rename <code>.exe</code> files as <code>.pdf</code> or <code>.jpg</code> to bypass naive filters.</p>
      <pre><code># Basic usage
file suspicious_document.pdf

# Example output for a disguised executable:
# suspicious_document.pdf: PE32 executable (GUI) Intel 80386, for MS Windows

# For a real PDF:
# report.pdf: PDF document, version 1.6

# Check multiple files at once
file *</code></pre>
      <p>Magic bytes to know:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>4D 5A</code> (<strong>MZ</strong>) → Windows PE executable</li>
        <li><code>7F 45 4C 46</code> (<strong>ELF</strong>) → Linux executable</li>
        <li><code>25 50 44 46</code> (<strong>%PDF</strong>) → Real PDF document</li>
        <li><code>50 4B 03 04</code> (<strong>PK</strong>) → ZIP archive (also .docx, .apk)</li>
        <li><code>D0 CF 11 E0</code> → Old Microsoft Office format (.doc, .xls)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>PE-bear</h3>
      <p><strong>PE-bear</strong> is a free, open-source portable executable editor and viewer. It presents the PE structure visually, making it ideal for analysts who are not yet comfortable reading raw hex. Think of it as a guided tour through the PE skeleton.</p>
      <h4>Key Features of PE-bear</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Import Table Viewer:</strong> Shows every DLL and function the malware imports. This is arguably the most informative section for quick triage — it reveals the malware's capabilities without reading a single byte of assembly.</li>
        <li><strong>Section Entropy Visualizer:</strong> Colour-codes sections by entropy level, instantly flagging packed regions.</li>
        <li><strong>Rich Header Analysis:</strong> Reveals the compilation toolchain, sometimes fingerprinting the compiler used (which can help with attribution).</li>
        <li><strong>Overlay Detection:</strong> Data appended after the end of the PE structure, often containing encrypted payloads or configuration files.</li>
        <li><strong>Resources Viewer:</strong> Malware frequently stores encrypted payloads as embedded resources (icons, version info, custom resource types).</li>
      </ul>

      <h4>Reading the Import Table — A Field Guide</h4>
      <table class="vt-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Suspicious Import</th><th>DLL</th><th>What it Suggests</th><th>Risk</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>CreateRemoteThread</code></td><td>kernel32.dll</td><td>Process injection / code injection</td><td><span class="badge red">HIGH</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>VirtualAllocEx</code></td><td>kernel32.dll</td><td>Allocating memory in another process</td><td><span class="badge red">HIGH</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>WriteProcessMemory</code></td><td>kernel32.dll</td><td>Writing shellcode into another process</td><td><span class="badge red">HIGH</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>RegSetValueEx</code></td><td>advapi32.dll</td><td>Registry persistence / configuration</td><td><span class="badge orange">MEDIUM</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>InternetOpen / WinHttpOpen</code></td><td>wininet/winhttp</td><td>C2 communication / data exfiltration</td><td><span class="badge orange">MEDIUM</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>CryptEncrypt</code></td><td>advapi32.dll</td><td>Encrypting data (ransomware?)</td><td><span class="badge red">HIGH</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>GetAsyncKeyState</code></td><td>user32.dll</td><td>Keylogging capability</td><td><span class="badge red">HIGH</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ShellExecute</code></td><td>shell32.dll</td><td>Launching programs, scripts</td><td><span class="badge orange">MEDIUM</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>GetTickCount</code></td><td>kernel32.dll</td><td>Time-based sandbox evasion</td><td><span class="badge green">LOW–MED</span></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="callout success">
        <div class="callout-label">💡 Pro Tip</div>
        A PE file with <em>very few imports</em> (like only LoadLibrary and GetProcAddress) is a huge red flag. It means the malware is dynamically resolving its API calls at runtime to hide its capabilities from static analysis. This is called <strong>dynamic API resolution</strong> and is a common packer technique.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 6 -->
    <section id="s6">
      <span class="section-num">06 —</span>
      <h2>Dynamic Analysis — Deep Dive</h2>
      <p>Dynamic analysis means running the suspect sample and watching what it actually does. While static analysis reads the map, dynamic analysis takes the journey. It is indispensable for analysing packed, polymorphic, or otherwise obfuscated malware that resists static examination.</p>
      <p>The core philosophy of dynamic analysis is <strong>controlled observation</strong>: let the malware do what it wants to do, but in a cage. Everything it touches — the registry, the filesystem, the network stack, other processes — is monitored and logged.</p>

      <h3>What Does Dynamic Analysis Reveal?</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Unpacked payload:</strong> Packers decompress their real code in memory at runtime — dynamic analysis captures this unpacked version</li>
        <li><strong>C2 infrastructure:</strong> Real network traffic shows command-and-control server addresses, protocols, and beacon intervals</li>
        <li><strong>Dropped files:</strong> Secondary payloads written to disk (stagers, modules, configuration files)</li>
        <li><strong>Persistence mechanisms:</strong> Scheduled tasks, registry Run keys, service installations, DLL hijacking</li>
        <li><strong>Lateral movement:</strong> SMB share enumeration, credential dumping (LSASS access), remote execution attempts</li>
        <li><strong>Data exfiltration:</strong> Files read, clipboard access, screenshot capture, keystrokes logged</li>
        <li><strong>Process tree:</strong> Which child processes the malware spawns and in what order</li>
      </ul>

