    <!-- Section 7 -->
    <section id="s7">
      <span class="section-num">07 —</span>
      <h2>Setting Up an Isolated Sandbox</h2>
      <p>The golden rule of dynamic analysis: <strong>never run malware on a machine you care about.</strong> The entire purpose of a sandbox is to give the malware a convincing-looking victim environment — while ensuring it cannot escape, phone home effectively, or persist anywhere meaningful.</p>

      <h3>What is a Sandbox?</h3>
      <p>A sandbox is an isolated, monitored execution environment. It typically consists of a <strong>virtual machine (VM)</strong> with all host–guest communication disabled, instrumented to capture every system call, API call, network packet, and file operation the malware performs. After analysis, the VM is reset to a clean snapshot.</p>

      <h3>Sandbox Options</h3>
      <div class="compare-grid">
        <div class="compare-card static">
          <h4>🖥️ Local Sandbox (Cuckoo)</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Full control over environment</li>
            <li>Customizable (add real documents, fake credentials)</li>
            <li>No data leaves your network</li>
            <li>Requires setup: VirtualBox + Cuckoo Sandbox</li>
            <li>Best for sensitive corporate analysis</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="compare-card dynamic">
          <h4>☁️ Cloud Sandbox</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Zero setup — upload and wait</li>
            <li>Any.run, Joe Sandbox, Hybrid Analysis</li>
            <li>Results in minutes with reports</li>
            <li>Caution: samples uploaded are not private</li>
            <li>Best for quick triage of non-sensitive samples</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Step-by-Step: Setting Up a Safe Analysis VM</h3>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">1</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h5>Install a Hypervisor</h5>
            <p>VMware Workstation Pro or VirtualBox. Create a Windows 10 VM. Take an initial clean snapshot before installing any tools.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">2</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h5>Install Monitoring Tools</h5>
            <p>Inside the VM: Process Monitor (Procmon), Process Hacker, Wireshark, Regshot (before/after registry snapshot), FakeNet-NG (simulates network to prevent real C2 contact).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">3</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h5>Configure Network Isolation</h5>
            <p>Set the VM's network adapter to "Host-Only" or use a dedicated VLAN. Optionally deploy FakeNet-NG to intercept and log DNS/HTTP without real outbound access.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">4</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h5>Defeat Anti-Analysis Checks</h5>
            <p>Rename the VM's hostname to something like "JOHN-PC". Set the username to a common first name. Install fake Office documents in Recent Files. Add fake browser history. Remove VMware Tools if using VMware (replaces with paravirtualization-minimal config).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">5</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h5>Take a Pre-Execution Snapshot</h5>
            <p>Before running any sample, snapshot the VM. After analysis, revert. This ensures every analysis starts from a known-clean state.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">6</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h5>Execute and Monitor</h5>
            <p>Start Procmon, take a Regshot baseline, start Wireshark — then run the sample. Let it execute for 3–5 minutes. Observe the console. Export all logs before reverting.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 8 -->
    <section id="s8">
      <span class="section-num">08 —</span>
      <h2>Observing Malware Behaviour</h2>
      <p>Once the malware is running, you're watching a crime in progress — in real time. Here's what to watch across the three main observation domains.</p>

      <h3>Registry Activity</h3>
      <p>The Windows Registry is the most common place malware establishes <strong>persistence</strong>. Use <strong>Regshot</strong> (compare before/after snapshots) or <strong>Process Monitor</strong> (filtered to RegSetValue operations) to catch changes.</p>
      <div class="tags">
        <span class="tag">HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run</span>
        <span class="tag">HKLM\System\CurrentControlSet\Services</span>
        <span class="tag">HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Winlogon</span>
        <span class="tag">HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce</span>
      </div>
      <p>These are the most common persistence registry locations. Any malware writing to these paths is establishing a mechanism to survive reboot.</p>

      <h3>Filesystem Activity</h3>
      <p>Monitor with Procmon filtered to <strong>WriteFile</strong> and <strong>CreateFile</strong> operations. Look for:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Files dropped in <code>%TEMP%</code>, <code>%APPDATA%</code>, <code>C:\Windows\System32\</code></li>
        <li>Files with misleading names (<code>svchost32.exe</code>, <code>windowsupdate.exe</code>)</li>
        <li>Files being renamed to <code>.locked</code>, <code>.encrypted</code> (ransomware indicator)</li>
        <li>Shadow copy deletion: <code>vssadmin delete shadows /all /quiet</code> (classic ransomware pre-encryption)</li>
        <li>Log file deletion in <code>C:\Windows\System32\winevt\Logs\</code></li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Network Activity</h3>
      <p>Capture with Wireshark or FakeNet-NG. Key observations:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>DNS queries:</strong> What domains is the malware resolving? DGA (Domain Generation Algorithm) malware produces dozens of nonsense DNS queries searching for its active C2.</li>
        <li><strong>HTTP/HTTPS beacons:</strong> Regular, time-interval requests to a C2 server. Look for unusual User-Agents, encoded data in GET parameters, or POST bodies with base64 payloads.</li>
        <li><strong>IRC / custom TCP:</strong> Older botnets used IRC for C2. Custom TCP protocols are still used by sophisticated malware.</li>
        <li><strong>SMTP activity:</strong> Spambots sending email using the victim's credentials or a hardcoded account.</li>
        <li><strong>Lateral movement:</strong> SMB connections to other local IP addresses (137/139/445), LDAP queries, RDP attempts.</li>
      </ul>
      <pre><code># FakeNet-NG captures all outbound traffic and responds with fake services.
# It logs all DNS queries, HTTP requests, and raw TCP connections.
# Example log entry:
[HTTP] GET /update.php?id=ABC123&amp;v=2.1.0 HTTP/1.1
Host: updates-cdn.example.com
User-Agent: Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0; Windows NT 5.1)
</code></pre>
      <div class="callout danger">
        <div class="callout-label">🚨 Red Flag</div>
        A User-Agent string like <code>Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0)</code> in 2024 is immediately suspicious — no real browser uses Internet Explorer 6. Hardcoded User-Agents are a common malware fingerprint.
      </div>
    </section>

