    <!-- Section 10 -->
    <section id="s10">
      <span class="section-num">10 —</span>
      <h2>Anti-VM &amp; Anti-Debug Techniques</h2>

      <h3>Anti-VM Techniques</h3>
      <p>Virtual machines leave many traces in the system. Sophisticated malware probes for these artifacts before detonating its payload. If any VM indicator is found, the malware exits silently, logs nothing suspicious, and the analyst gets a false clean result.</p>
      <h4>Common VM Indicators Checked by Malware</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Registry keys:</strong> <code>HKLM\SOFTWARE\VMware, Inc.\VMware Tools</code>, <code>HKLM\SOFTWARE\Oracle\VirtualBox Guest Additions</code></li>
        <li><strong>Process names:</strong> Scanning running processes for <code>vmtoolsd.exe</code>, <code>vboxservice.exe</code>, <code>vmsrvc.exe</code>, <code>wireshark.exe</code>, <code>procmon.exe</code> (analysis tools themselves)</li>
        <li><strong>Hardware fingerprinting:</strong> MAC addresses with VMware OUI prefix (00:0C:29), CPUID hypervisor bit, disk model strings containing "VBOX" or "VMWARE"</li>
        <li><strong>Screen resolution:</strong> Sandboxes often use 800×600 or 1024×768. Malware checks for unusually small resolutions.</li>
        <li><strong>User activity simulation:</strong> Checking mouse cursor movement. If the cursor hasn't moved in 60 seconds, it's probably a sandbox — no human analyst moves their mouse every second.</li>
        <li><strong>Uptime checks:</strong> Newly spawned VMs have very short uptimes. Malware may wait until uptime exceeds a threshold before activating.</li>
        <li><strong>File/path checks:</strong> Looking for <code>C:\analysis\</code>, <code>C:\sandbox\</code>, or specific Cuckoo agent paths.</li>
      </ul>
      <pre><code>; Assembly example of CPUID hypervisor check
mov eax, 1
cpuid
test ecx, 0x80000000   ; Hypervisor Present bit
jnz  vm_detected       ; Jump if running in a VM
; ... proceed with malicious code
</code></pre>

      <h3>Anti-Debug Techniques</h3>
      <p>When a human analyst attaches a debugger (OllyDbg, x64dbg, WinDbg) to examine malware step-by-step, the malware may detect this attachment and alter its behaviour — crashing, entering an infinite loop, or triggering a decoy code path to waste the analyst's time.</p>
      <h4>Common Anti-Debug Methods</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>IsDebuggerPresent():</strong> The simplest check. The PEB (Process Environment Block) has a <code>BeingDebugged</code> flag set to 1 when a debugger is attached. Malware checks this and exits.</li>
        <li><strong>CheckRemoteDebuggerPresent():</strong> Checks if a remote debugger (like OllyDbg as a separate process) is attached.</li>
        <li><strong>NtQueryInformationProcess:</strong> A more thorough native API check that bypasses some debugger-hiding tools.</li>
        <li><strong>Timing attacks:</strong> Using <code>GetTickCount()</code> or <code>RDTSC</code> (CPU timestamp counter) to measure instruction execution time. Debuggers slow execution dramatically. If a block of code takes 10× longer than expected, a debugger is present.</li>
        <li><strong>Exception-based detection:</strong> Deliberately triggering a structured exception (SEH). Debuggers intercept exceptions; if the malware's custom handler is not called, a debugger is present.</li>
        <li><strong>Hardware breakpoint detection:</strong> Reading the CPU debug registers (DR0–DR7) via GetThreadContext. Active hardware breakpoints betray a debugger.</li>
        <li><strong>Parent process check:</strong> Checking whether the parent process is explorer.exe (normal launch) vs a debugger's PID. A process launched from a debugger has the debugger as its parent.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="callout success">
        <div class="callout-label">🛡️ Analyst Counter-Measure</div>
        Tools like <strong>ScyllaHide</strong> (OllyDbg/x64dbg plugin) automatically patch all common anti-debug checks, making the debugger invisible to the malware. This is called "debugger hiding" and is a standard part of any serious analysis setup.
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 11 -->
    <section id="s11">
      <span class="section-num">11 —</span>
      <h2>Obfuscation &amp; Packing</h2>
      <p>Obfuscation is the art of making code hard to understand. Packing is a specific, extremely common form of obfuscation where the entire malware executable is compressed or encrypted and wrapped in a small loader stub. When executed, the stub decompresses/decrypts the real malware into memory and runs it.</p>

      <h3>Types of Obfuscation</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>String obfuscation:</strong> Strings (URLs, registry keys, API names) are XOR'd, base64-encoded, AES-encrypted, or split into fragments that are only assembled at runtime.</li>
        <li><strong>Control flow obfuscation:</strong> Inserting useless code (dead code), adding fake conditional branches, using opaque predicates (conditions that always evaluate the same way but look complex).</li>
        <li><strong>API hashing:</strong> Instead of importing function names directly (visible in the import table), malware walks the export table and looks up functions by their hash. The strings "CreateThread" never appears anywhere in the file.</li>
        <li><strong>Polymorphism:</strong> The malware rewrites its own code each time it replicates, changing byte patterns to defeat signature-based detection while keeping the same logical behaviour.</li>
        <li><strong>Metamorphism:</strong> More advanced — the malware completely rewrites its own logic engine, not just its byte pattern. Extremely difficult to detect with signatures.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Packing — The Most Common Obfuscation</h3>
      <p>Packing is so common that analysts check for it immediately. The most popular packer is <strong>UPX</strong> (Ultimate Packer for eXecutables), an open-source tool originally designed for legitimate size reduction but heavily abused by malware authors. However, hundreds of custom packers exist.</p>
      <div class="compare-grid">
        <div class="compare-card static">
          <h4>🔍 Detecting Packing</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>High entropy in .text or unusual sections (&gt;7.0)</li>
            <li>Very few imports (only LoadLibrary / GetProcAddress)</li>
            <li>Section names like UPX0, UPX1, .packed</li>
            <li>Small import table but large file size</li>
            <li>PE-bear entropy viewer flags red sections</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="compare-card dynamic">
          <h4>📦 Defeating Packing</h4>
          <ul>
            <li>Run it — the packer stub unpacks the payload into memory</li>
            <li>Use a debugger: set breakpoint after OEP (Original Entry Point)</li>
            <li>Dump memory to disk with PE-sieve or Scylla</li>
            <li>For UPX: <code>upx -d malware.exe</code> unpacks automatically</li>
            <li>Use automated unpackers (Unpac.me, online tools)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 12 -->
    <section id="s12">
      <span class="section-num">12 —</span>
      <h2>Reading a VirusTotal Report</h2>
      <p><strong>VirusTotal</strong> is a free web service owned by Google that analyses suspicious files and URLs with over 70 antivirus engines simultaneously. It is often the analyst's first stop and is genuinely one of the most powerful free tools available. Knowing how to read a VT report correctly is a critical analyst skill — because misreading it leads to wrong conclusions.</p>

      <h3>Submitting to VirusTotal</h3>
      <p>You can submit a file directly or search for its hash. <strong>Prefer submitting the hash</strong> if the sample is sensitive — file submissions are retained by VT and may be shared with security vendors. Hash lookup gives you the same result without uploading the file.</p>

      <h3>Anatomy of a VirusTotal Report</h3>
      <table class="vt-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Section</th><th>What It Shows</th><th>How to Use It</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Detection Ratio</strong></td><td>X/72 engines flagged it</td><td>Even 1/72 deserves investigation if it's a major vendor (Kaspersky, CrowdStrike, Sophos)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Detection Names</strong></td><td>Engine-specific malware names</td><td>Cross-reference names: Trojan.GenericKD = generic; "Raccoon Stealer" = specific family ID</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>File Details</strong></td><td>Hash, size, type, compile time</td><td>Compile timestamp — note it can be forged; check import hash (imphash) for family clustering</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Behavior Tab</strong></td><td>Sandbox run results</td><td>MITRE ATT&amp;CK technique tags, network activity, registry changes — most valuable section</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Relations Tab</strong></td><td>Contacted IPs/domains/URLs</td><td>Pivot to infrastructure: same C2 used by other malware samples?</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Community Tab</strong></td><td>Analyst comments</td><td>May contain family attribution, decryption keys, or links to blog analysis</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Similarity</strong></td><td>Structurally similar files</td><td>Find other samples in the same campaign or from the same threat actor</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>Common Misinterpretations</h3>
      <div class="callout danger">
        <div class="callout-label">🚨 Don't Fall for These Traps</div>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>0/72 does NOT mean clean.</strong> A brand-new, zero-day sample with no prior submissions will score 0/72. Check the first-submission date — very recent = suspicious.</li>
          <li><strong>High detection doesn't equal high severity.</strong> A potentially unwanted program (PUP) may score 40/72 while actual ransomware scores 5/72 (because it's new).</li>
          <li><strong>Detection names are not standardised.</strong> "Trojan.Agent" from one engine means almost nothing. Look for specific, named families across multiple vendors.</li>
          <li><strong>Old scan results.</strong> VT shows the most recent scan date. If a file was last scanned 6 months ago, re-scan it to get current verdicts.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h3>The Behaviour Tab — Gold Mine</h3>
      <p>The Behaviour tab aggregates results from multiple sandbox runs (Cuckoo, CAPE, Triage, VirusTotal's own sandbox). Look for MITRE ATT&amp;CK technique IDs here. Common ones that indicate danger:</p>
      <div class="tags">
        <span class="tag">T1055 — Process Injection</span>
        <span class="tag">T1083 — File &amp; Directory Discovery</span>
        <span class="tag">T1486 — Data Encrypted for Impact</span>
        <span class="tag">T1071 — Application Layer Protocol (C2)</span>
        <span class="tag">T1547 — Boot/Logon Autostart Execution</span>
        <span class="tag">T1082 — System Information Discovery</span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 13 -->
    <section id="s13">
      <span class="section-num">13 —</span>
      <h2>Interpreting Sandbox Output</h2>
      <p>Sandbox reports can be dense and overwhelming for new analysts. Here is a systematic approach to cutting through the noise and extracting actionable intelligence.</p>

      <h3>The Triage Framework — READ Order</h3>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">R</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h5>Registry Changes</h5>
            <p>Immediately check for Run key modifications — this tells you if malware persists across reboots. Note any unusual software keys being created or modified.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">E</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h5>Execution Tree (Process Tree)</h5>
            <p>Trace the parent-child process chain. Malware.exe spawning cmd.exe spawning powershell.exe is a classic execution chain for script-based loaders. Injected code shows as unusual parent-child relationships.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">A</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h5>API &amp; System Calls</h5>
            <p>Scan the API call log for high-value targets: CreateRemoteThread, VirtualAllocEx, OpenProcess (with full access rights), RegSetValue, CreateService. These narrate the malware's story in technical detail.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="step-num">D</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <h5>DNS / Network Indicators</h5>
            <p>Export all contacted domains and IPs. Cross-reference with threat intel (VirusTotal, Shodan, abuse.ch). DNS queries to DGA-like domains (random-looking names) indicate a botnet with domain generation.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>A Sample Cuckoo Report — Annotated</h3>
      <pre><code>// CUCKOO SANDBOX REPORT EXCERPT — Annotated
{
  "info": {
    "score": 9.2,           // Severity 0-10; 9.2 = almost certainly malicious
    "duration": 120          // Seconds of execution observed
  },
  "signatures": [
    "antivm_vbox_files",     // ← Checked for VirtualBox files (evasion attempt)
    "antidbg_windows",       // ← Called IsDebuggerPresent() (anti-debug)
    "persistence_run",       // ← Wrote to HKCU\...\Run (persistence!)
    "network_cnc_http",      // ← Made HTTP request consistent with C2 beacon
    "ransomware_files"       // ← File renaming pattern matches ransomware
  ],
  "network": {
    "dns": [{"hostname": "xf3k9mq2.top"}], // ← Looks like DGA domain
    "http": [{
      "uri": "/gate.php",
      "method": "POST",      // ← POST = likely data exfiltration or registration
      "user_agent": "Mozilla/4.0 (compatible; MSIE 6.0)" // ← Hardcoded, fake UA
    }]
  },
  "dropped_files": [
    "C:\\Users\\user\\AppData\\Roaming\\svchost32.exe" // ← Dropper! Note fake name
  ]
}</code></pre>
      <p>Reading this output in 60 seconds, an analyst knows: <strong>the sample is ransomware with C2 capabilities, uses domain generation, drops a secondary payload, and actively tries to evade both sandboxes and debuggers.</strong> This is a high-priority, high-severity threat.</p>

      <h3>Writing Your Analysis Report</h3>
      <p>Every analysis should conclude with a brief report containing: SHA-256 hash, malware family (if identified), capabilities observed, network indicators (IPs, domains), host-based indicators (file paths, registry keys, mutex names), and a MITRE ATT&amp;CK mapping. This report becomes a detectable artefact — your incident response team uses it to hunt for the malware across the entire network.</p>
    </section>

